2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,47,2075,15087,387,279,8381,2326,47311,448,198,59,7265,90,6612,9,532,47,7,16,8,14581,1124,839,220,16,11,90155,47,7,17,8,14581,1124,839,220,17,11,90155,47,7,18,8,14581,1124,839,220,18,11,90155,47,7,19,8,14581,1124,839,220,19,624,59,408,90,6612,9,92,12209,400,47,7,20,15087,646,387,13302,304,279,1352,400,32,1124,839,1124,37018,90,33,15170,34,2137,3,1380,400,32,4779,400,33,4779,323,400,34,3,525,6785,25780,11,323,400,34,3,374,10250,13,220,7379,400,32,488,425,488,356,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $P(x)$ be the degree three polynomial with\n\\begin{align*}\nP(1) &= \\log 1, \\\\\nP(2) &= \\log 2, \\\\\nP(3) &= \\log 3, \\\\\nP(4) &= \\log 4.\n\\end{align*}Then $P(5)$ can be expressed in the form $A \\log \\frac{B}{C},$ where $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are positive integers, and $C$ is prime.  Find $A + B + C.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"881d4085036bc81db82fbcace4d9384cedd2dcfb687302ae48f914d39756361e"},"seconds":62.490153789520264,"seed":169090584820470608,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for $P(5)$ given the conditions on $P(x)$, we need to recognize that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree three and takes on the values $\\log 1, \\log 2, \\log 3, \\log 4$ at $x=1, 2, 3, 4$, respectively. This means that the polynomial $Q(x) = P(x) - \\log x$ has roots at $x=1, 2, 3, 4$. Since $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial, $Q(x)$ must be a cubic polynomial minus $\\log x$, and for it to have four roots, $Q(x)$ must be identically zero. Thus, we have:\n\n\\[ P(x) = \\log x + R(x) \\]\nwhere $R(x)$ is a cubic polynomial that has roots at $x=1, 2, 3, 4$. However, since $R(x)$ is cubic, it cannot have four roots unless it is the zero polynomial. Therefore, $R(x) = 0$ and we have:\n\n\\[ P(x) = \\log x. \\]\n\nHowever, this seems to contradict the fact that $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial. Instead, we need to consider the Lagrange interpolation formula to find $P(x)$ explicitly. The polynomial $P(x)$ can be written as:\n\n\\[ P(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^4 \\log i \\cdot \\ell_i(x), \\]\nwhere $\\ell_i(x)$ are the Lagrange basis polynomials defined as:\n\n\\[ \\ell_i(x) = \\prod_{\\substack{1 \\leq j \\leq 4 \\\\ j \\neq i}} \\frac{x-j}{i-j}. \\]\n\nWe need to find $P(5)$:\n\n\\[ P(5) = \\log 1 \\cdot \\ell_1(5) + \\log 2 \\cdot \\ell_2(5) + \\log 3 \\cdot \\ell_3(5) + \\log 4 \\cdot \\ell_4(5). \\]\n\nCalculating each $\\ell_i(5)$:\n\n\\[ \\ell_1(5) = \\frac{(5-2)(5-3)(5-4)}{(1-2)(1-3)(1-4)} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}{-1 \\cdot -2 \\cdot -3} = -1, \\]\n\\[ \\ell_2(5) = \\frac{(5-1)(5-3)(5-4)}{(2-1)(2-3)(2-4)} = \\frac{4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}{1 \\cdot -1 \\cdot -2} = 4, \\]\n\\[ \\ell_3(5) = \\frac{(5-1)(5-2)(5-4)}{(3-1)(3-2)(3-4)} = \\frac{4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1}{2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot -1} = -6, \\]\n\\[ \\ell_4(5) = \\frac{(5-1)(5-2)(5-3)}{(4-1)(4-2)(4-3)} = \\frac{4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2}{3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = 4. \\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ P(5) = \\log 1 \\cdot (-1) + \\log 2 \\cdot 4 + \\log 3 \\cdot (-6) + \\log 4 \\cdot 4 = 0 + 4 \\log 2 - 6 \\log 3 + 4 \\log 4. \\]\n\nSince $\\log 4 = 2 \\log 2$, we have:\n\n\\[ P(5) = 4 \\log 2 - 6 \\log 3 + 4 \\cdot 2 \\log 2 = 4 \\log 2 - 6 \\log 3 + 8 \\log 2 = 12 \\log 2 - 6 \\log 3 = 6 (2 \\log 2 - \\log 3) = 6 \\log \\frac{4}{3}. \\]\n\nSo, $P(5) = 6 \\log \\frac{4}{3}$. Here, $A = 6$, $B = 4$, and $C = 3$. Therefore, $A + B + C = 6 + 4 + 3 = 13$.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
